// EXPECT: E0251 — a destructuring statement whose target count differs from the result count
//
// **Exact arity** (ADR-0052 §2), and the alternative is what makes it worth a corpus file: Jai lets a
// caller bind a *prefix* of the results, which means adding a result to a procedure silently changes
// nothing at any call site, and *reordering* results silently changes what every existing caller
// binds. That is action at a distance — the same objection ADR-0014 §3 made about import order
// deciding behaviour, and ADR-0048 §3 about an `#import` redefining `+`.
//
// Filed under `imports/invalid/` rather than `type-errors/` because that directory's contract is that
// its files "parse, lower and resolve cleanly" and are rejected by *semantic analysis*. This is a
// sema diagnostic, so it would qualify — except that the harness there asserts the exact code set per
// file, and the three refusals below are cleaner to keep together than to split across three files
// whose only difference is a count.
divide :: (a: s64, b: s64) -> (s64, bool) {
    return a / b, true;
}

/// Too few names for two results.
too_few :: () -> s64 {
    q := divide(7, 2);
    return q;
}

/// Too many names for two results.
too_many :: () -> s64 {
    q, ok, extra := divide(7, 2);
    return q;
}

/// A destructuring statement on a procedure returning **one** value, which is `x := f();` and needs
/// no target list. Named precisely, because "expected 2 values" without saying what it does return
/// sends the reader looking for a problem at the call site.
single :: (n: s64) -> s64 {
    return n;
}

wrong_form :: () -> s64 {
    a, b := single(1);
    return a;
}
